{
  "dom_id": "step-e85",
  "name": "Example: Determining Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions by Factoring the Numerator and Denominator",
  "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-5x+6}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+3)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-3 = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=3.\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
